10. What is the Docker daemon?

    The Docker Daemon (dockerd) is the background service responsible for managing Docker images, containers,
    networks, and volumes. The Docker CLI sends commands to the daemon through the Docker API, and the daemon
    performs the requested operations.

11. What is OCI?
    OCI stands for Open Container Initiative. It is an open-source project that defines industry standards for
    container images and container runtimes.
    The main purpose of OCI is to ensure that containers and container images follow common standards and are
    not tightly tied to a particular container platform.

12. What is the difference between a Docker Image and a Docker Container?
    A Docker Image is a read-only template that contains the application code, runtime, libraries, dependencies,
    and configuration required to run an application. A Docker Container is a running instance of that image.
    We create a container from an image, and multiple containers can be created from the same image.

13. How do you Dockerize a Spring Boot Application?
    To Dockerize a Spring Boot application, I first build the application JAR using Maven or Gradle.
    Then I create a Dockerfile that uses a Java runtime image, copies the JAR into the image,
    and starts it using java -jar. After building the Docker image with docker build,
    I can run the application using docker run.